Take part in Astro Pi to ignite young people’s interest space and enhance their programming skills

Hi De,

The European Astro Pi Challenge is back for the 2026/27 season, and we’re excited to announce that registration is officially open for both Mission Zero and Mission Space Lab!

Here is everything you need to know about this year’s updates and key dates.

Mission Zero: Art in Orbit 🎨

In Mission Zero [https://www3.raspberrypi.org/e/800983/mission-zero/4w38fb/2754813312/h/n1Jf5ypOtVnq5004D2I4W7p6fHgUk_YUadvK1lr49uA, young people design nature-inspired pixel art to display for astronauts on board the ISS as they go about their daily tasks. They will learn Python fundamentals—like working with variables—and use the Astro Pi's colour sensor to adapt their artwork dynamically.

What’s new for mentors this year:

Fresh Inspiration: Explore new starter code examples selected directly from standout 2025/26 Mission Zero entries.

Worked Examples: Our Mission Zero project guide now includes two worked examples showing students how to code a static image or build an animated sequence.

Updated Mentor Guide: Packed with practical classroom resources, troubleshooting tips, and quick fixes for common errors.

NOTE: You will be given a new classroom code for Mission Zero 2026/27 when you sign in to the Mission Hub. [https://www3.raspberrypi.org/e/800983/login/4w38ff/2754813312/h/n1Jf5ypOtVnq5004D2I4W7p6fHgUk_YUadvK1lr49uA]

Mission Space Lab: Real Space Science 🛰️
In Mission Space Lab [https://www3.raspberrypi.org/e/800983/mission-space-lab/4w38fj/2754813312/h/n1Jf5ypOtVnq5004D2I4W7p6fHgUk_YUadvK1lr49uA, teams of 2 to 6 young people tackle a real scientific investigation in space. This year, your teams can design their own original space science experiment or follow one of our ready-made project guides to get started.

Students will write a Python program to log sensor or camera data to a file, with every eligible program guaranteed a 10-minute flight slot on the ISS.

Testing &amp; Tools:
Teams can test their code online using the web-based Astro Pi Replay tool [https://www3.raspberrypi.org/e/800983/-/4w38fm/2754813312/h/n1Jf5ypOtVnq5004D2I4W7p6fHgUk_YUadvK1lr49uA Teams can access using Chrome, Safari, or Firefox to see real historical ISS sensor data, images, and error logs. Offline testing is also available via the Thonny IDE plugin.

 📅 Key Dates for 2026/27

14 September 2026: Mission Zero and Mission Space Lab launch

1 October 2026, 15:00 (CEST) / 16:00 (BST): Getting started with Mission Space Lab livestream. Join here. 

22 February 2027 (12:00 CET): Mission Space Lab program submissions close

22 March 2027: Mission Zero submissions close

April–May 2027: Approved programs run on board the ISS

June 2027: Teams receive official data sets and certificates

June 2027: Exclusive live Q&amp;A for MSL teams with an ESA human space flight expert.

We can’t wait to see what you and your teams create this year! Head over to astro-pi.org to get started.

Best wishes,

Astro Pi Mission Control

https://www3.raspberrypi.org/e/800983/ESAEducation-/4w38fq/2754813312/h/n1Jf5ypOtVnq5004D2I4W7p6fHgUk_YUadvK1lr49uA

